sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch11-11> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The same arctic and alpine species on widely separated mountain summits and in arctic regions are relicts of populations that spread across the intervening lowlands during the Glacial period and were stranded on mountains and in the far north as the climate warmed.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Identical alpine species on distant mountains are relicts of the Glacial period";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XI, “Geographical Distribution”; section “Dispersal during the Glacial period co-extensive with the world”. Scope: arctic-alpine plants and animals of Europe and North America"
}
